## 1. INTRODUCTION
Despite international prohibition by the Chemical Weapons Convention (CWC), the number of incidents involving chemical warfare agents (CWAs; chemicals designed to kill or disable exposed persons by altering their physiological systems) has increased in recent years.1−3 Therefore, materials capable of the safe and efficient capture or degradation of chemical warfare agents (CWAs) are critically important in the modern age. While weaponized chemicals are intended as direct threats to human life, human activities, ranging from the mining and consumption of fossil fuels to the industrial production of desired chemicals, also result in the release of toxic and harmful chemicals (e.g., $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ $\mathrm{NO}_{x},$ ${\cal S}\mathrm{O}_{x},$ CO, $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ etc.) to the atmosphere, threatening quality of human life. These gases also relevant to several major environmental issues that our society is facing today, such as global warming, acid rain, and ozone depletion, in addition to damaged ecological systems.4,5 Furthermore, toxic industrial chemicals (TICs) are also produced and transported worldwide as chemical feedstocks to many indispensable chemical commodities such as fertilizers. The prevalent usage of TICs entails a higher risk of accidental spills and leaks of these toxic chemicals, often on large scales; therefore, protection for employees who work with TICs is essential.
Capturing TICs from the atmosphere presents a significant challenge due to their low atmospheric concentrations, which are often in the parts-per-million (ppm) range, as well as the presence of competing molecules such as water from moisture.
Hence, the development of materials with a highly selective uptake capacity of TICs under ambient conditions remains a great challenge. Currently, activated carbon is used as a cheap adsorbent in filters employed for protection against both CWAs and TICs; however, activated carbon can only provide weak interactions with toxic chemicals, resulting in very low uptake capacities, especially at low concentrations of toxic chemicals.7 Furthermore, protection against polar toxic chemicals is even less effective for these carbon-based materials due to their nonpolar surfaces. To increase the affinity for and uptake capacity of multiple toxic chemicals at low concentrations, activated carbon is blended with metal-salts, such as zinc, copper, silver, molybdenum, or basic organic molecules such triethylenediamine or 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane.8,9 However, these carbon-based composite materials lack long-range order (i.e., crystallinity) and compositional tunability, resulting in significant challenges regarding the design of and improvements to targeted systems for toxic gas adsorption applications. To this end, metal−organic frameworks (MOFs), atomically precise hybrid materials that are synthesized via the selfassembly of metal cations or clusters and organic linkers, offer a unique platform to potentially address these limitations due to their high crystallinity and great synthetic versatility.10−13 Owing to the vast array of different metal nodes and organic linkers available, researchers can access MOFs with a variety of topologies in addition to a broad range of chemical and physical properties.14−19 While reticular chemistry20,21 allows for the design of targeted frameworks from their components, the crystalline nature of MOFs has enabled the use of predictive modeling, which has played an important role in the discovery of novel MOF-based materials for many applications, particularly for adsorbent discovery.22−24
Because of their modular nature, MOFs are uniquely poised to answer many important questions in the scientific community, ranging from catalysis and gas separations to sensing and water capture/purification, that have been challenging to address with traditional porous materials such as zeolites or activated carbons.25−29 Among these areas of research, reports detailing the use of MOFs for the capture and storage of toxic gases have been relatively scarce despite the importance of this topic, potentially resulting from concerns about safety, the need for specialized instrumentation, and comprehensive personnel training required for performing analyses of these systems.30,31
For CWAs in particular, researchers circumvent the use of extremely toxic compounds by working with surrogates that mimic the most important chemical and physical properties of the actual agents while demonstrating relatively lower toxicities. These results are then validated in collaboration with a certified laboratory.
Additionally, finding sorbents that remain intact under highly corrosive environment has comprised another significant challenge in the past decade.32−34 With the discovery of MOFs that exhibit high chemical stabilities, there has been an increasing body of work reported on the topic.30,35,36 Commonly, open metal sites on the nodes of the MOFs have been utilized as strong adsorption sites for Lewis basic gases, even at low concentrations.37 Moreover, the incorporation of functional groups on organic linkers enabled the tuning of physical properties, including hydrophobicity and the electron donating or accepting ability, as well as to engender selective reactivity toward targeted gases. Finally, the modification of pore sizes and environments has resulted in the enhanced selectivity and uptake capacity of targeted gases through the ability to provide interactions between the analyte and the multisites inside the of pores.
## 2. CAPTURE OF CHEMICAL WARFARE AGENTS AND SIMULANTS
## 2.1. Organophosphorous Agents and Their Simulants
Legacy stockpiling and devastating use of chemical warfare agents (CWAs) against populations have served as persistent threats since their inception in WWI, requiring viable detoxification strategies.47,48 Lethal organophosphorus-based CWAs (Figure 1, Table 1) inhibit acetylcholinesterase, which, in turn, prevents muscle contractions, leading to asphyxiation.49 Technologies to protect military personnel and innocent civilians from nerve agent exposure, detect organophosphorus species, and dispose of large CWA stockpiles have been widely explored by porous materials that feature additional analyte adsorption sites such as activated carbons and metal oxides.50,51 However, selective uptake of such organophosphorus nerve agents by such aforementioned materials is hampered by nonuniform pore sizes and limited chemical tunability. The tunability and high surface areas of MOFs prompted their investigation as sorbents for efficient uptake of CWAs.30−32,35,52 Beyond the adsorption of CWAs, the chemical detoxification of nerve agents has been shown to proceed through a hydrolysis reaction by Lewis acidic catalysts.53 Such knowledge has enabled the design of MOF-based catalysts to detoxify such agents, and the reader can be informed with the following work and reviews.35,38,40,41,54,55 Thus, we will discuss efforts toward the synthesis and design of MOFs for the uptake of organophosphorus compounds and accompanying mechanistic adsorption studies that have furthered sorbent design. The emergence of promising MOF-based CWA sensors and implementation of MOFs on fabric for protective equipment highlights the possible devices that can be designed and utilized.
The selective adsorption of organic phosphonates in MOFs was first explored by Ni et al. in MOF-5/IRMOF-1 and compared to other commonly used, amorphous toxic vapor sorbents, Tenax TA and Carbotrap.58 Tenax TA is a porous polymer resin derived from 2,6-diphenylene oxide, while Carbotrap is a carbon-black-based sorbent. The sorption capacity of dimethylmethyl phosphonate (DMMP), a less toxic simulant of such phosphonate-based agents, was $0.95{\ g\ g}^{-1}$ of MOF-5. Only $0.0\bar{13}\ \mathrm{g}\ \bar{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and $0.02\textrm{g}\overline{{\mathsf{g}}}^{-1}$ of DMMP was adsorbed per gram of Tenax and Carbotrap, respectively. Importantly, preconcentration gains were measured to be greater than 5000 for DMMP when using MOF-5 over a sample time of $^{4}\:s,$ while Tenax TA exhibited a gain of 2. Moreover, selective adsorption by MOF-5 was demonstrated with an observed slow adsorption of dodecane with a preconcentrator gain of only 5. Comparison to a substrate more similar in molecular weight or functionality to DMMP could be more indicative of the MOF’s efficacy within this context. Concern regarding the implementation of MOFs within atmospheric conditions and the subsequent competitive adsorption of water prompted the computational screening and experimental design of hydrophobic MOFs for the remediation of toxic chemicals.59,60 To circumvent the facile hydrolysis of $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ bonds that can render MOF-5 moisture sensitive, the use of imidazolebased linkers formed $\scriptstyle{[Z\ n_{4}\mathrm{O}}$ (3,5-dimethyl-4-carboxypyrazola$\mathbf{to}{\rangle}_{3}].$ , referred to as 1, with similar topology yet greater stability due to the presence of highly robust $\mathrm{{Zn-N}}$ bonds.59 The utilization of variable temperature pulse gas chromatography revealed a high retention time for the warfare agent simulant diisopropylfluorophosphate (DIFP) and a poor retention time for water by the more hydrophobic MOF. Furthermore, calculated zero-coverage adsorption heats indicated favorable interactions between DIFP and 1 $(44.8\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}})$ and a low affinity of the framework for water vapor $(17.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.$ ). A high adsorption of DIFP $\left(60\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}\right)$ , as determined through breakthrough experiments conducted under $50\%$ relative humidity, confirmed that the presence of water vapor expected under atmospheric conditions does not limit uptake of the simulant. DIFP capture was also probed within a hydrophilic MOF, HKUST-1, and a commercially available activated carbon with comparable surface area to the novel hydrophobic Zn MOF of interest. Upon exposure to ambient moisture, the open metal sites were quickly hydrated in HKUST-1, preventing DIFP uptake, while the activated carbon displayed comparable selectivity to 1 of agent uptake over water vapor. Thus, the modularity afforded within MOFs can design such hydrophobic MOFs with noteworthy CWA uptake.
Beyond tuning the metal-linker bond strength, linker modification through the incorporation of functional groups has been shown to influence the adsorption of organophosphorus agents within MOFs. Previously, our group demonstrated the efficacy of linker functionalization for affecting CWA catalytic detoxification with UiO-66, UiO-67, and NU1000 analogues.39,61 A recent systematic study was carried out by Ruffley et al. that modeled and calculated the binding energies of DMMP with eight different functionalized UiO-67 MOFs (Figure 2).62 The respective strongest, intermediate, and weakest binding energies were identified as $\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3},$ and $-\mathrm{H}$ functionalized UiO-67, which were experimentally probed as well (Figure 2b). Low defects in the samples precluded any chemisorption of the adsorbed species, as evidenced by temperature-programmed desorption (TPD) experiments and minor shift in the phosphoryl stretching frequency (Figure 2d). The temperature at which the strongly physisorbed DMMP desorbed from the functionalized MOF was greatest for $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ followed by ${\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ and last $-\mathrm{H},$ where experimentally derived energies of desorption matched well with those predicted by DFT calculations. By envisioning a stratified $\mathbf{MOF}^{63}$ of UiO-67- NH2 ⊂ UiO-67-CH3 ⊂ UiO-67, DMMP equilibrium concentrations within $\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ $\mathrm{-CH}_{3},$ and $-\mathrm{H}$ strata were predicted in a ratio of 55:17:1.
Besides tailoring linker functionality, the long-range order of MOFs enables the incorporation of guest entities within MOF pores. To further modulate agent capture, the encapsulation of a polyoxometalate (POM) capable of favorable H-bonding with DMMP agent within a Cu-based MOF formed NENU-11 $\mathrm{\langleH_{3}[(C u_{4}\check{C}l)_{3}(B T C)_{8}]_{2}[P W_{12}O_{40}]\cdot(C_{4}H_{12}N)_{6}\cdot3H_{2}O)}$ .64 An uptake of ${\sim}15.5$ molecules of DMMP was observed per $\mathrm{Cu}_{4}$ within NENU-11, greater than that of HKUST-1 or MOF-5 when normalized to the formula unit, $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}_{4},$ respectively (Figure 3). Moreover, NENU-11 outperformed NENU-3, which is HKUST-1 containing $\mathrm{[PW_{12}\mathrm{\hat{O}_{40}}]^{3-}}$ . The authors postulated that the unsaturated Cu site present in the unique SBU of NENU-11 facilitated the high DMMP uptake, in addition to H-bonding with the POM. Interestingly, the parent MOF framework could not be synthesized without the use of Keggin-type POMs, suggesting the anionic complex templates the growth of the sodalite topology MOF. Nonetheless, the enhanced uptake of DMMP by NENU-3 per formula unit as compared to HKUST-1 indicated the direct role of the POM in simulant capture. Adsorption profiles of the toxin in the presence of varying relative humidity suggested that water vapor competed with the agent for binding to NENU-11. Nonetheless, the first proof of concept work showed that guest incorporation can be explored to tailor MOF efficacy for the uptake of organophosphorus simulant agents.
The observed efficacy of open metal sites (OMS) within the POM composite demonstrated a further strategy to improve
MOF uptake of CWAs. While the first examples of MOF-based sorbents were mainly driven by microporosity and weak framework interactions, the tunability of MOFs can access frameworks with OMS as predesigned, specific binding sites without the need for a guest that occupied valued pore space. Such design was explored by Zhao et al.65 in the synthesis of a novel trinuclear $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{Ca}$ -based MOF with the $\scriptstyle\alpha-\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$ (apo) topological net (Figure 4). Both $Z\mathrm{n}$ sites present in the cluster are coordinatively unsaturated to facilitate for the binding of nerve agent simulant methylphosphonic acid (MPA). Highperformance liquid chromatography was utilized to obtain MPA adsorption isotherms, which indicated an adsorption density of $3.42\ \mathrm{\bar{mmol/g}}$ and outperformed previously measured adsorption densities.66 Periodic density functional theory calculations probed the adsorption interactions of MPA with the MOF, which supported binding of the simulant at a bridging site at the open $Z\mathrm{n}$ sites. Such collaborations between experimental and theory are enriched by the well-defined structure of MOFs to precisely identify the adsorption site.
Impressive mechanistic insights of CWA adsorption have been derived from experimental observations, but the inherent danger of conducting experiments with nerve agents limits academic research. Therefore, the predictive power of computational modeling is critical for the design of nerve agent sorbents. MOF uniformity and periodicity mesh well for computational and experimental collaboration.67 To identify possible MOFbased sorbents for the capture of soman and sarin, Matito
Martos et al. identified 1647 MOFs from the CoRE database that possessed favorable electrostatic interactions for CWA adsorption and appropriate pore diameters to accommodate CWAs.68 Adsorption of simulants (DIFP and DMMP) and CWAs (soman and sarin) onto the MOFs under dilute conditions was modeled to calculate isosteric heats of adsorption and Henry coefficients (Figure 5). The largest and most favorable Henry coefficients were obtained with MOFs containing a largest cavity diameter (LCD) of about 5 Å, yet MOFs with largest cavity diameters (LCDs) greater than 8 Å generally showed a decrease in CWA/simulant interaction and lower Henry coefficients. The 156 hydrophobic MOFs were then selected to limit water competition between CWA and water molecules at the active site. The loading capacities were calculated; the top 8 MOFs with highest CWA capacity were then targeted for grand canonical Monte Carlo simulations with the lethal concentrations of sarin, soman, and mustard gas to inform experimental breakthrough testing utilizing a mustard gas simulant. In general, the authors noted similar trends in the heats of adsorption of CWA agents and simulants onto MOFs, yet observed variation in the calculated absolute values inspired further comparisons of agent and simulant reactivities.69 Agrawal et al. modeled the adsorption of nerve agents (sarin and soman) and simulants (DIFP, DMMP, DCP, and DMNP) onto 3000 CoRE MOFs under dilute conditions.70 DMNP gave the highest agreement for soman capture, whereas DMMP and DCP were better representatives of sarin (Figure 6). A strong correlation between the heat of adsorption and the Henry’s constant suggested the applicability of the observed interactions at very low concentrations. Maurin and co-workers devoted their simulation studies to water stable ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ and $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ frameworks for the adsorption of soman and sarin as well as DIFP and DMMP.71 Of note, some of the studied MOFs achieved CWA adsorption enthalpies higher than $-100\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}.$ , greater than any experimental adsorption enthalpies observed to date.59 Moreover, they indicated excellent agreement of uptake values calculated for soman and pinacolyl methylphosphonate (PMP), a less explored nerve agent simulant.
Effective strategies for designing MOFs for CWA capture and mechanistic insights derived from in situ experimental observations and computational modeling prompted the development of promising MOF-based devices to detect and combat CWAs. High affinity observed within $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ -based MOFs for organophosphorus agents motivated the development of MOF-based sensors for such species.72,73 A robust, amino functionalized MOF, UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ that is based on a $\mathrm{[}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\mathrm{]}^{12+}$ cluster, was implemented in chemically sensitive field effect transistors (ChemFETs).74 Within ChemFETs, analyte adsorption alters the gate electrode work function, which is indicated by measuring the contact potential difference (CPD) relative to a reference electrode (Figure 7). Comparable to previously measured DMMP adsorbents,75 the $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -based sensor reported an average response time $t_{\mathrm{r}90}$ and decay time $t_{\mathrm{d}90}$ of $1.9\pm1.3\mathrm{\min}$ and $5.7\pm3.5\ \mathrm{min},$ respectively. Strong affinity of the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster for the DMMP simulant resulted in analyte desorption from the framework as a rate limiting process. Impressively, the device reported an extrapolated limit of detection (LOD) of $0.3\pm0.1\mathrm{{\ppb}}$ , one of the lowest LOD reported for a Kelvin probe gas sensing-based device; the long-range order of MOF adsorption sites enabled such detection at ppb concentrations. Despite an excess of water relative to DMMP by about five orders of magnitude, the high affinity of the $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ framework for the simulant resulted in CPD responses under $50\%$ RH, similar to those observed under dry conditions. The composite material’s selectivity for DMMP and stability under a more realistic, humid environment rendered the device suitable for real world applications.
## 2.2. Mustard Gas (HD) and Its Simulants
First used during World War I, bis(2-chloroethyl)sulfide, also known as mustard gas, sulfur mustard, and HD, is still considered the most effective chemical weapon due to ease with synthesis and dispersion as well as its incapacitating ability. Unfortunately, this highly toxic chemical is still in use, particularly in the Middle East against civilian and military populations, so the development of materials to efficiently absorb and detoxify HD is still necessary. Currently, metalimpregnated activated carbons are used for adsorption of a wide spectrum of toxic chemicals, but their amorphous nature leads to nonuniform pore sizes/geometries, less tunability, slow diffusion, and low capacities when used as filtration cartridges in gas masks. To improve upon this technology, MOFs, with their highly tunable and crystalline nature, have been examined as faster, higher capacity alternatives. Because of safety and security concerns, few studies report on using chemical warfare agents (CWA) because they can only be used in approved government laboratories. Instead, researchers use simulants or chemically similar compounds with significantly lower toxicity to test materials and to inform upon principles to design materials with improved performances. Simulants for HD include diethyl sulfide (DES), tetrahydrothiophene (THT), and 2-chloroethyl ethyl sulfide (CEES). Some reports claim dimethyl sulfide as a simulant for HD; however, notable size differences bring into question the accuracy of such studies in predicting the behavior of the material with the real CWA. Differences in experimental setups (i.e., flow rates, amount of material, observed areas) hinder broad comparisons of absorptive behavior between materials, but few have attempted computational calculations to assist in material selection.68 While reports of materials for fully/partially reactive HD decontamination exist, namely oxidation, hydrolysis, or dehalogenation, $^{55,76-78}$ here we will focus on adsorbent materials to replace the currently employed metal-impregnated activated carbon filters79 or create new classes of protective equipment.
Hydrophobic MOF materials are typically targeted for capture of hydrophobic sulfur mustard and its simulants. Early work compared a $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF ( $\mathrm{{Zn}_{4}O}$ (3,5-dimethyl-4- carboxypyrazolato)3) to the hydrophilic MOF HKUST-1, and the activated carbon material, Carboxen.59 The $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF performed comparable to Carboxen when DES adsorption was monitored through pulse gas chromatography and breakthrough experiments. HKUST-1 under dry conditions, however, had a greater heat of adsorption. Despite the higher DES heat of adsorption in HKUST-1 than the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF and the high sulfide to water heats of adsorption ratio in HKUST-1, Cu-based HKUST-1 was unable to retain any DES under humid conditions. This underscores the importance of considering ambient moisture when designing materials for protective equipment. In a similar follow-up study,80 the same group designed an isoreticular series of $\mathbf{Ni}_{8}$ -based MOFs. The functionalization of the linker with ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ groups had a positive effect on the adsorption of DES. Notably, due to the hydrophobic nature of the ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ functionalized MOF, the DES adsorption was unaffected by humidity.
Not only do MOFs offer a breadth of possible structures, sizes, and functionalities but also for each MOF, various synthetic routes exist, often giving rise to different topological, morphological, or “defective” outcomes. In one case, the CuTCPP MOF in a typical synthesis gave rise to 2D sheets that stack like plates as they aggregate on substrates (Figure 8a). When Parsons and co-workers employed a hydroxy double salt solid metal source, euhedral needle-like $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCPP crystals were formed on a $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ -modified polypropylene fiber (Figure 8b).81
The euhedral MOF crystals dramatically increased the surface area of the composite to $220~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ from the $<10~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for the anhedral-containing composite. Microbreakthrough experiments showed that the CEES adsorption capacity for CuTCPP powder did not diminish as a result the directional growth on the fiber material under both wet and dry conditions (Figure 8c,d).
Because of their exceptional hydrolytic ability toward nerve agents, $^{38,82-84}\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOFs are a stable platform onto which to install functional groups for protection against multiple agents. The $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and MOF-808, have been deposited on fibers, specifically polyester, for CWA simulant protection.85 We developed a template-free, hydrothermal synthesis procedure for preparing MOF/fiber composites, where initially a coordination polymer was cast onto the fiber as nucleation sites for MOF growth. In less than $^{2\mathrm{h},}$ these nucleation sites converged into a continuous MOF layer (Figure $\mathfrak{g}_{\mathtt{a}-\mathtt{c}})$ . Permeation of CEES was measured over a fabric swatch. The unmodified polyester material offered no protection, with near instantaneous permeation. The UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ /fiber, with a loading of 27 wt $\%$ MOF, exhibited negligible permeation up to $28~\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 9d). The MOF-808/fiber further increased the permeation time to $68~\mathrm{\min}$ . Both materials sloped up to maximum permeation around $700\mathrm{min}$ , suggesting the materials could offer partial protection over time.
An important consideration when designing materials for protective equipment is the balance between moisture vapor transport rate (MVTR) and adsorption capacity. The ideal material would be able to “breathe”, allowing for moisture to pass and reduce the burden on the wearer while still offering sufficient protection against toxic chemicals. For this reason, mixed matrix membrane (MMM) and mixed membrane composite (MMC) with MOF components have been investigated. In one report by Peterson, Epps, and co-workers, a MOF/polymer bilayer composed of SEBS/HKUST-1 $(50\%)$ on $\mathrm{SEBS/UiO}–66–\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(50\%)$ $\left[S{\mathrm{EBS}}\right.\ =$ polystyrene-blockpoly(ethylene-ran-butylene)-block-polystyrene] was used to test the MVTR and permeation of CEES (Figure 10a).86 The MMC exhibited limited permeation of CEES over $16\mathrm{h}$ (Figure 10b). Notably, against the HD, no breakthrough occurred even after $2500\ \mathrm{min}$ using this MMC. The MVTR of this composite was $16~\mathrm{g/m}^{2}/\mathrm{h}$ . The defects in the MOF structure allowed for improved access of CEES to the Cu binding sites while simultaneously allowing water to transmit through the composite. In a similar study, SIS/HKUST-1 $\left[S\mathrm{I}S=\right.$ polystyrene-block-polyisoprene-block-polystyrene] MMC was used to test MVTR and CEES permeation.87 Breakthrough times increased as HKUST-1 loading increased up to $50\%$ , and doubling the thickness of the wet film enhanced the breakthrough time approximately four-fold (Figure 11a,b). Microbreakthrough experiments showed CEES had decreased diffusivity as MOF content increased and as MMC thickness increased. MVTR for this film increased from 1 to $8\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{h}$ for materials containing 0 to 50 wt $\%$ MOF content, respectively (Figure 11c). The mechanical properties of the composites were also assessed, and the study concluded that intermediate MOF loadings between 20 and 33 wt $\%$ balanced the mechanical robustness with simulant absorption and MVTR. These results highlight important consideration when developing MMCs and suggest that MMCs are promising next-generation materials for protective equipment.
The ever-growing number of known and possible MOF structures has necessitated well-informed materials selection for targeted applications. The atomically precise structure of MOFs allows simulations to assist in selecting materials possessing the most promising target properties. In the study mentioned in the previous nerve agent section, the MOF database was also evaluated for their utility in selective HD adsorption over water in addition to organophosphorus agents.68 Evaluation of water uptake is crucial because the presence of humidity often competes for the same binding sites as the target CWAs, and due to higher water vapor concentration, the CWAs could be poorly adsorbed. Of the 2932 MOFs, 1647 MOFs possess the geometric pore/apertures capable of this adsorption process. After a Widom insertion to evaluate the water affinity, 156 of the most hydrophobic MOFs were subjected to GCMC simulations. Four of the simulated MOFs calculated to have the highest CWA loading, and one was selected for experimental testing. The $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{BTP})_{2}$ $\mathbf{\widetilde{B}}\mathbf{TP}=1,3,5$ -tris(1H-pyrazol-4-yl)benzene)88 exhibited $0.6\mathrm{mol/kg}$ uptake of the mustard gas simulant, DES, which corresponds well to the calculated $0.617{-}1.193\mathrm{mol/kg}$ at the relative DES pressures were used in the breakthrough experiment $\left(0.01{-}\bar{0}.1\ \mathrm{Pa}\right)$ . Despite the hydrophobic nature of the MOF, water cophysisorbed with DES but did not affect the MOF structure. This screening approach highlights some of the considerations necessary for CWA capture.
Despite the colloquial term, mustard gas is a liquid under ambient conditions, as are the organophosphonate-based nerve agents. Consequently, many reports for CWA abatement/ detoxification occur in the solution-phase. The high chemical stability of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -based MOFs allows for mechanistic studies for CWA absorption in aqueous environments. UiO-67 and NU1000 were investigated for the removal of CEES from aqueous solution.89 From kinetic parameters calculated form ICP-AES results, the two MOFs were determined to perform comparably when assessing CEES adsorptive capacities $\mathrm{(4.197{\mmo}{\bar{l}}/g}$ and $4.000\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ for NU-1000 and UiO-67, respectively). The pseudo-first-order kinetics in both cases suggests that the simulant interacts with the MOF via hydrogen bonds from the chlorine and sulfur atoms to the $_{\mathrm{Zr-OH}}$ groups of the MOF. This study establishes important design rules for MOF-based CWA adsorbents.
## 3. CAPTURE OF TOXIC INDUSTRIAL CHEMICALS (TICS)
## 3.1. Hydrogen Sulfide $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S})$
Hydrogen sulfide is an impurity commonly encountered in the oil and gas industries. The highly toxic (Table 2) and corrosive nature of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ has prompted the development of materials with both high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ affinity for protective equipment and high recyclability for separations processes. Using MOFs for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ abatement allows for the precise tuning of binding/active site location at the spatially separated nodes or linkers. The high modularity of MOFs also provides opportunity for the addition or incorporation of functional groups that would selectively promote interaction with the target molecule. The crystallinity of the MOFs permits the optimization of these materials assisted by computational methods, sparing many hours of bench work. Materials for detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{,}}$ , both in the atmosphere and in physiological conditions, have been extensively reported on and reviewed.93 Such materials typically operate as fluorescence probes using nitro, alkene, and azide functional groups or as chemoresistive sensors. While several reports demonstrate the ability of MOFs to catalyze the oxidation or reduction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ to sulfate or elemental sulfur, respectively,94−97 and these types of materials have been used in industrial applications, this review will focus on adsorbents. Capture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ using MOFs has been r e v i e w e d p r e v i o u s l y b y u s a n d o t h e r s t h r o u g h 2017. $^{30-32,52,98-101}$ Here, we highlight more recent advancements and provide our present outlook on the field.
Prior to the use of gas streams such as refinery-off-gas, natural gas, syngas, and biogas, in energy generation and industrial processes, impurities must be removed. In particular, the components of acid gas, Lewis acidic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},$ often lead to corrosion in downstream processes. The stability of the adsorbent under these acidic gas conditions is critical for the efficiency and reuse of the material.102−105 Often materials have higher affinity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ , so their concurrent presence leads to competitive adsorption and necessitates the use of multiple materials to remove them from a gas stream. A better methodology is to remove simultaneously both $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ using a material with a selectivity ratio approximately equal to 1 to prevent the formation of other impurities such as carbonyl sulfide, which presents further complex processing challenges for the purification of natural gas.106 To this end, Eddaoudi and co-workers targeted a series of fluorinated MOFs (NbOFFIVE$\mathrm{1-Ni,}$ AlFFIVE-1-Ni, SIFSIX-2−Ni-I, and SIFSIX-3-Ni) with varying pore volumes.106 SIFSIX-2−Ni-I had a large pore volume, and despite the high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ affinity, $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ condensed in the pores and the material achieved a high ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}/{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ selectivity ratio. To increase charge density, SIFSIX-3-Ni was employed. The contracted pore volume allowed for greater interaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with the framework and subsequently a much lower ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}/$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity ratio. The selectivity closely matched that of previously reported selectivity of NbOFFIVE-1-Ni. To increase $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ affinity, the isostructural Al-analogue of NbOFFIVE-1-Ni, namely AlFFIVE-1-Ni (Figure 12a), was employed. This MOF offers distinct adsorptive sites, where the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ could interact with the open Al metal sites and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ could interact with the fluorine moieties. AlFFIVE-1-Ni achieved the desired 1:1 selectivity ratio for ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{:CO}}_{2}$ in the presence of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and was stable and recycled over a range of temperatures and gas concentrations (Figure 12b). This MOF was also incorporated into a mixed matrix membrane $\left(\mathbf{MMM}\right)^{107}$ and used in a membrane-based separations.108 These studies highlight systematic design principles to fine-tune MOF properties toward their desired functionality.
The selective removal of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in natural gas streams is often difficult to achieve due to competitive adsorption at Lewis basic sites. To understand properties that give rise to high/reversible H2S adsorption, Zou and co-workers109 evaluated 11 common MOF-based materials using DFT calculations, molecular dynamics simulations, and breakthrough experiments. These MOFs were then categorized into three groups: reversible physisorption, Cu- or $Z\mathbf{n}$ -containing, and redox active. We note that due to the high crystallinity of MOFs, the design of materials for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ capture is well informed by in silico calculations.110−117 The maximum uptakes were obtained from breakthrough experiments using $1\%H_{2}S$ in He. All MOFs except Mg-MOF-74, MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ , UiO-66, and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ lost most of their capacity after the first cycle. These MOFs possess open metal sites and high surface areas but suffer from low ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}/{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ selectivity (Figure 13). Ce-BTC and ZIF-8, while avoiding corrosion and possessing reversible sorption, have limited uptake due to lack of open active sites. MOFs containing a Cu or $Z\mathrm{n}$ , $\mathrm{Cu-BDC}(\mathrm{ted})_{0.5},$ Zn-MOF-74, Cu-BTC, and MOF5, easily and irreversible were demonstrated to bind $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},$ forming metal sulfides and subsequently damaging the structure. These MOFs exhibit high uptake and selectivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , but are not recyclable, limiting future applications. In the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S,$ , the ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{III})$ centers in MIL-100(Fe) gel (also described at Fe-BTC) were reduced to ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{II})$ , concomitant with the oxidation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ to $S_{8}$ . The oxidative ability of the MOF gel is intriguing for catalytic abatement of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ but underexplored in this study. Dependent on the application, the integrity of the material may or may not be essential.
The highest $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption of any material with reversibility was reported for MIL-53(Al)-TDC $[\mathrm{TDC}~=~2,5\$ -thiophenedicarboxylate],118 first reported by Tschense et al.119 In a $5\mathrm{vol}\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ feed, MIL-53(Al)-TDC took up 18.13 mmol ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S/{\mathrm{g}}$ at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 bar. PXRD patterns and SEM images confirmed that the structure is stable to $\mathrm{H}_{2}S_{\mathrm{.}}$ , and adsorption−desorption isotherms were cycled without diminishing the capacity for at least five cycles (Figure 14). To probe the uptake mechanism, Monte Carlo simulations were performed. The calculations revealed that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ binds favorably with the two $\scriptstyle\mu-\mathrm{OH}$ on the nodes, with another $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ molecule, and with the thiophenebased linker. The precise spatial arrangement of these interactions is only allowed by the crystallinity of the MOF structure. Further, the structural elucidation using simulations is a great advantage MOFs have over other typical adsorbents, and as such, the structure can be even further optimized. We note that the effect of water vapor, which is present in industrial gas streams, was not explored in this study.
To avoid MOF degradation through binding of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ on open metal sites, Walton and co-workers120 identified ligand functionalization as a viable strategy to enhance adsorption without compromising stability. Three MOFs with varying pore sizes, UiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr})$ , MIL-125(Ti), and MIL-101(Cr), were used to determine the impact of amine functionalized linkers on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ separation from natural gas simulant mixtures. The pelletized MOFs were used in a fixed-bed adsorption experiments to conduct breakthrough experiments. Of note, the MIL-101 samples suffered some structural deformation during the pelletization process, which requires further optimization of the pelletization conditions for the industrial practicality of this MOF. As pore size increased from MIL-125 to UiO-66 to MIL101, the effect of the amine functionalization diminished (Figure $_{15\mathrm{a-d})}$ , with MIL-101 and MIL- ${\mathrm{101-NH}}_{2}$ having comparable breakthrough times and adsorption capacities. This effect could be due to the smaller pores in MIL-125 creating a confinement effect or the orientation of the amines along the pore walls as a result of the topology. DFT calculations, although possibly thanks to the crystalline nature of MOFs, were not utilized to elucidate the binding mechanism. MIL- ${\mathsf{125-N H}}_{2}$ exhibited the highest removal performance of 0.559 mmol $\mathrm{H}_{2}S/\mathrm{g}$ in $99\%\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixture. In simulant gas mixtures containing $10\%\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ a roll-up effect for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was observed in the microporous frameworks. This feature indicates the replacement of weaker adsorbates with stronger ones, meaning that $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ has preferential binding over $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to the frameworks at the concentrations used for this study. The addition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the feed stream led to greater competition of adsorption sites in the smaller pore frameworks, with MIL-101 and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ having comparable breakthrough curves (Figure $\mathbf{\sigma}_{15\mathrm{e-g}},$ ) due to reduced competition with sorption capacity of $0.44~\mathrm{mmol}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}/{\mathrm{g}}$ . While this study highlights the importance of using multicomponent mixtures to screen material performances, the influence of water vapor and potential contaminants was not evaluated. Another interesting point is that the UiO-66 in this report performed differently than in previously published results. This difference was attributed to differences in defect sites arising from the different synthetic protocols and should be considered when selecting MOFs; this correlation of defect density (modulated by synthetic conditions) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ absorption has been systematically studied in MIL-101.121
Benign syntheses of previously published MOFs for use as $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption were recently reported; however, the chosen MOFs either have comparatively low uptakes122 or suffer from instability/reactivity under $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ stream preventing recyclability.123 The green synthesis of MOFs with high $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption and stability is desirable. Future studies should also aim at installing functionality of protective equipment. Promising work by Mirica and co-workers used MOF and COF coated conductive fabrics as both a sensor and adsorbent for toxic gases like $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ and NO.124 First, however, discrepancies in material stability and reversibility, as in HKUST-1, need to be resolved to identify superior materials for H2S capture.125
## 3.2. Ammonia $(N H_{3})$
With an annual global production of about 176 million tons,31,36,126 ammonia is one of the most important industrial gases due to its use in applications ranging from the production of fertilizers to the development of both pharmaceutical and chemical feedstocks. However, the high toxicity of ammonia warrants public concern about pollution: the immediately dangerous to life or health air concentration (ILDH) value of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is $300\ \mathrm{ppm}$ (Table 2).30,126 Generally, the two most significant sources of ammonia pollution originate from industrial ammonia spills and from farming (e.g., from fertilizers, as well as urine and dung generated by livestock). Upon entering the atmosphere, ammonia can react with $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ or $\mathrm{SO}_{x},$ both of which are present in the atmosphere from fossil fuel emissions and generate particulate matter less than $2.5\mu\mathrm{m}$ wide $(\mathrm{PM}_{2.5})$ that can potentially lead to detrimental environmental consequences. Reducing ammonia emissions is thought to be the most effective approach decrease the amount of $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ in the atmosphere.127 Specifically, the use of porous sorbent materials for ammonia capture is believed to a practical solution for ammonia pollution; however, the current commercial adsorbent filters, which are derived from activated carbon, suffer from low selectivity and low capacity for ammonia.37 Therefore, there is an urgent need for the development of selective, efficient, and cost-effective sorbents with high capacity to improve current technology of adsorbent filters used for the capture of ammonia.128−136
As a result of their permanent porosities, exceptional surface areas, and large degree of tunability at the molecular level, MOFs have been investigated as adsorbents for various gases and vapors.20,137−140 Specifically, MOFs can be tailored to incorporate a diverse range of Lewis or Brønsted acidic sites, which hold great promise for the efficient capture of ammonia, a basic gas molecule.37,129,131,133−135,141−146
In a pioneering study, Yaghi and co-workers examined ammonia capture by a series of MOFs via kinetic breakthrough measurements,37 finding that MOFs with functional groups (e.g., IRMOF-3, an amino-functionalized version of MOF-5) or open metal sites (e.g., MOF-199 or MOF-74) have much higher dynamic adsorption capacities than unfunctionalized MOFs, such as MOF-5 or MOF-177, despite the much higher porosities and surface areas of the unfunctionalized MOFs (Figure 16). In the following years, researchers examined both strategies (i.e., functionalized linkers and the use of open metal sites) in the context of ammonia capture in MOFs.
Inspired by the effectiveness of MOFs with open metal sites for ammonia capture, our group studied kinetic breakthrough experiments of ammonia in Cu-MOF-74 (also known as CuCPO-27),147 which has rich open metal sites and one of the highest densities of $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ sites $\left(4.7~\mathrm{nm}^{-3}\right)$ of known MOFs. These properties led to the outstanding volumetric uptake of ammonia under both dry and humid $\mathrm{RH}=80\%$ ) conditions; however, similar to HKUST, Cu-MOF-74 loses structure integrity due to the breaking of Cu-carboxylate bonds inside the structure.148
While breakthrough experiments provide kinetic and dynamic performance data for MOFs used for ammonia capture, sorption measurements offer thermodynamic isotherm data via ammonia adsorption and desorption studies. For example, Dinca and coworkers performed ammonia sorption studies on a series of isostructural, mesoporous triazolate-based MOFs,149 $\mathrm{M}_{2}C\mathrm{l}_{2}\mathrm{(BTDD)}\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O)}_{2}^{\mathrm{-}}$ $\mathrm{\big(M=Mn,\Co,}$ and Ni),131 revealing that activated samples of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{M_{n_{2}}C l_{2}}(\mathbf{BTDD})\left(\mathbf{H_{2}O}\right)_{2},}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{(BTDD)(H}_{2}\mathrm{O)}_{2}^{\cdot},$ and $\mathrm{Ni_{2}C l_{2}(B T D D)(H_{2}O)_{2}}$ adsorb 15.47, 12.00, and $12.02\ \mathrm{\mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{~K},$ respectively (Figure 17 and Table 3). Notably, these values for ammonia uptake are higher than the corresponding value for UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ under analogous conditions. Interestingly, ammonia uptake is reversible over multiple cycles despite the presence of open metal sites in these MOF materials. Additionally, the smaller pore versions (e.g., $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathbf{BBTA})$ ; $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{\cdot}$ , Co, and Ni) have higher capacities, especially at low pressures, for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ than the $\bar{\bf M_{2}C l_{2}}(\bar{\bf B T D D})$ series of $\dot{\bf M}{\bf O}{\bf F}s^{133}$ due to the higher density of open metal sites in the former series. Finally, dynamic breakthrough measurements of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ revealed that $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ (BBTA) outperforms its larger pore analogue, $\mathrm{Co_{2}C l_{2}(B T D D)}$ . While $\mathtt{C u}$ analogue showed very promising equilibrium capacity at ambient conditions, the low stability renders it less appealing compared other metal analogues for this particular application.
In addition to the excellent ammonia capture performance of MOFs with open metal sites, MOF materials with Brønsted acidic binding sites also present a promising platform for ammonia capture. Rosseinsky and colleagues reported the acidloaded $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{(OH)}_{2}\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP)}$ (Al-PMOF), which contains infinite rod SBUs, for ammonia capture using kinetic microbreakthrough experiments,150 finding that both the HCl- and formic acid-loaded versions of Al-PMOF show better performance for ammonia capture than the parent Al-PMOF. This proof- of-concept study highlights the use of MOFs as hosts for Brønsted acidic guests as a promising host−guest system for the basic ammonia capture.
As an alternative strategy for incorporating Brønsted acidic sites into MOFs for ammonia capture, MFM-300(Al), constructed from $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -biphenyl-tetracarboxylate and infinite Al rod SBUs (Figure 18), demonstrates reversible ammonia adsorption performance with the uptake $15.7\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 bar and $2{\bar{7}}3\operatorname{K}.$ .151 After 50 adsorption−desorption cycles, no loss of crystallinity and adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ was observed, indicting the stability of this material in the presence of ammonia. Furthermore, in situ neutron powder diffraction and synchrotron FTIR microspectroscopy were employed to study the host−guest interaction between ammonia and MOF materials, revealing a pseudochemisorption binding mechanism for ammonia adsorption in MFM-300(Al).
The coordination environment of Brønsted acidic sites (i.e., bridging OH groups) in a porous MOF could also be vital for performance as ammonia sorption materials. Recently, our group studied ammonia sorption in a series of isoreticular MOFs with rod secondary building units (SBUs)154 (i.e., Al-PMOF, Ga-PMOF, and In-PMOF), which are constructed from onedimensional rod-packing chains $\left(\left[\mathrm{M(OH)(-COO)_{2}}\right]_{\infty}\right)$ and a porphyrinic organic linker (TCPP). As revealed by multicycle $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ sorption experiments, these MOFs exhibited promising performance toward the ammonia capture (Figure 19). Additionally, diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) was employed to study the adsorption mechanism of ammonia in this series of MOFs, revealing a decrease in the intensity of $-\mathrm{OH}$ stretch vibrations and the formation of peaks corresponding to the $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ cation under the adsorption process. These studies provide insight into the chemical bonding interaction between $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ gas and the bridged − OH sites within these rod-based MOFs.
In addition to sorption performance, the stability of MOFs under ammonia exposure could be important for some applications related to ammonia capture that require the longterm utilization of porous materials. Kitagawa et al. systematically studied the stability of MOFs upon ammonia exposure using PXRD measurements,156 illustrating that MOFs constructed from oxophilic metal cations, such as $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ , and ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ , are more stable than MOFs made from labile cations, such as ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ , when exposed to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ gas. Additionally, it was found that MOFs containing anionic nitrogen donors, such as
aPrimary standards provide public health protection, and secondary standards provide public welfare protection (i.e., damage to animals, crops, vegetation, and buildings). These two national ambient air quality standards were set by the US EPA. Units of measure for the standards are parts per million (ppm) by volume, parts per billion (ppb) by volume.
ZIF-8, are more stable in the presence of ammonia than MOFs composed of neutral nitrogen donors. Finally, ammonia sorption measurements revealed that MOFs with open metal sites, such as $\mathbf{MOF-}74(\mathbf{Mg}).$ , are highly adsorptive, while MOFs with Brønsted acidic sites, such as MIL-53(Al), are moderately adsorptive, and MOFs without functional groups, such as ZIF-8, are not adsorptive.
## 3.3. Sulfur Dioxide $(\mathsf{S}\mathsf{O}_{2})$
Sulfur dioxide $(\mathsf{S O}_{2})$ is a highly toxic byproduct of fossil fuel emissions and significant atmospheric pollutant that can react with ammonia to form hazardous $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ products. While desulfurization technologies, such as limestone-scrubbing and the use of aqueous alkaline solutions, can remove up to $95\%$ of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from flue gases in power plants,157,158 trace amounts of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ still present in exhaust gases pose series health risks to the public, in addition to deleterious effects on the environment (Table 4).30 Therefore, the capture of trace $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (down to $400\ \mathrm{ppm},$ ) from flue gas is crucial for the control of air quality. Here, we review the recent progress regarding porous MOFs used for $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ capture that represent a promising class of materials for the effective removal of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in flue gas.30,159−164
In one of the early works on MOFs used for $S O_{2}$ adsorption, Yang et al. studied the mechanism of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption in an Albased MOF, NOTT-300, using in situ powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), inelastic neutron scattering studies, and density functional theory (DFT) calculations.159 NOTT-300, also known as MFM-300(Al), is constructed from biphenyl$_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylate and one-dimensional chains of $\left[\mathrm{AlO}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\right]$ . This study revealed the coexistence of modest hydrogen bonding interactions between $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules and the hydroxyl groups on Al-SBU, as well as weak cooperative interactions between $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and adjacent $\mathrm{C-H}$ groups present on benzene rings from ligands. At 1 bar, NOTT-300 shows a high $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ uptake of 8.1 and $7.1\ \mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}}$ at 273 and $^{298}\mathrm{~K},$ respectively. MFM-300(In), an analogue to NOTT-300 with Inrod $S\mathrm{BUs},$ displays an enhanced capacity of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ uptake of 8.3 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 20).160 Combination of inelastic neutron scattering, X-ray diffraction, and DFT calculations revealed the binding modes of the $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ guest molecule inside the porous MOF host (Figure $20\mathsf{a}-\mathsf{c}$ ). Multiple interactions with $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules resulted in $\mathrm{SO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 5000. The outstanding performance of these materials toward
$S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ capture makes them potential candidates for the selective removal of trace $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from flue gas.
The strong interaction between $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and the host framework can potentially induce a phase change in the MOF. For example, NOTT-202a, composed of single ionic nodes $([\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}]),$ and biphenyl- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetra-phenyl-4-carboxylate linkers, is less stable toward $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption compared to the MFM-300 series.166 NOTT-202a undergoes an irreversible structural transition to NOTT-202b upon $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption, indicated by triple $\pi^{\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi}$ interactions between the ligands from two catenated frameworks that were observed after shifting to the second network. At 1 bar, NOTT-202a shows a very high $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $\sim13.6$ and ${\sim}10\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 268 and $298\mathrm{~K},$ respectively (Table 5).
Similar to MFM-300, which contains bridging $-\mathrm{OH}$ sites, $\mathbf{Zr}.$ - MOFs also contain a large amount of accessible $-\mathrm{OH}$ sites (both terminal and bridging hydroxyl sites), which could be useful for $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ capture.
More recently, MFM-601, a $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF with 8-connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ nodes, has been reported to exhibit a high $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $12.3\mathrm{mmolg}^{-1}$ at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{K}.^{167}$ MFM-601 is obtained from a phase transition of MFM-600, which is synthesized from 4- connected ligands and $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ nodes. When MFM-600 is heated to $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the presence of HCl and DMF, a method generally used to remove the capped benzoate groups on $\mathrm{Zr}$ nodes, a phase transition is triggered via postsynthetic ligand removal. Mechanistic studies revealed that the dipole moment of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ enhances intermolecular interactions during adsorption, in addition to the strong $S O_{2}$ -hydroxyl interactions (Figure 21). Highest $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ occupancy was observed near terminal hydroxyls of $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster resulting in intermolecular distances reported in solid ${\mathrm{~SO}}_{2},$ which translated into high uptake capacity for $S O_{2}$ .
While the nodes of MOFs can be utilized as strong adsorption sites, metal cations can also be incorporated into the porous host material via postmodification to improve $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ capture. Navarro and co-workers reported the introduction of $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ on the defective sites of a nickel pyrazolate-based MOF, resulting in enhanced interactions between the MOF and $S O_{2}$ (Figure 22).168 Dynamic breakthrough experiments revealed that the $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ -exchanged MOFs adsorb more $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ than either the parent MOF or the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ -exchanged MOF. Additionally, this study revealed that extra-framework $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ cations inside the MOF perform better than barium hydroxide for the capture of ${\mathrm{~\thinspace~}}S{\mathrm{O}}_{2},$ illustrating the importance of the accessibility of the active adsorption sites.
Tan et al. employed a combination of experimental and theoretical methods to investigate the mechanism of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption into two microporous MOFs, $\mathrm{M}\mathrm{(BDC)}(\mathrm{TED})_{0.5}$ 1 $\mathbf{\dot{M}=N i}$ and $Z\mathbf{n}$ ; $\mathrm{BDC}=1{,}4$ -benzenedicarboxylate; TED $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ triethylenediamine).169 At ${\sim}1$ bar and 298 K, $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})$ - $(\mathrm{TED})_{0.5}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{(BDC)}\mathrm{(TED)}_{0.5}$ exhibit values for $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ uptake of 9.9 and $4.4\mathrm{mmol}_{\mathrm{}}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , respectively. Furthermore, under these conditions, $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{BDC})(\mathrm{TED})_{0.5}$ outperforms Mg-MOF-74, which adsorbs $8.6~\mathrm{mmol~g}^{-1}$ of $S O_{2}$ . PXRD experiments reveal that $\mathrm{Ni(BDC)(TED)}_{0.5}$ retains its crystallinity, while $\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{(BDC)}$ - $(\mathrm{TED})_{0.5}$ partially decomposes after $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ sorption. Infrared spectroscopic data and results from ab initio DFT calculations concurrently support multiple interactions between the $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ guest molecules and the MOF hosts.
The influence of functional groups on the performance of $S\mathsf{O}_{2}$ adsorption in MOFs in humid conditions, which is an important consideration for adsorbent materials employed for the purification of flue gas, was highlighted by Mounfield et al. In this study, a titanium-based MOF, MIL-125, exhibits a lack of stability in the presence of humid $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ due to the hydrolysis of titanium carboxylate bonding, while MIL-125 remains intact when exposed to either water vapor or dry ${\mathrm{~\thinspace~}}S{\mathrm{O}}_{2},$ respectively.170 $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ an amino-functionalized isoreticular analogue to MIL-125, was found to be stable toward humid $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ due to the higher energy barrier for Ti−O hydrolysis after functionalization which prevents the side reactions with newly formed acidic species in the presence of water (Figure 23). Both MIL-125 and $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{MIL-}125\mathbf{\tilde{\mathbf{-}}N H_{2}}}\end{array}$ adsorb about ${\sim}9.5$ mmol $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{\Lambda}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{2}}\end{array}$ at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and reveal single gas adsorption isotherms.
Microporous fluorinated MOFs comprise an alternative class of promising candidates for efficient $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption. Cui et al. reported a series of anion-pillared hybrid porous materials for $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ capture,161 in which coordinated networks based on the 6- connected pcu net are built from nitrogen donor-based organic linkers and inorganic hexafluorosilicate anions $(\operatorname{SiF}_{6}^{2-})$ , known as the SIFSIX series.138,172−175 SIFSIX-1- ${\cdot}\mathrm{Cu}^{176}$ and SIFSIX-2- $\mathrm{{Cu-i}^{174}}$ are composed of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dipyridylacetylene linkers ( $\mathbf{\ddot{i}}^{\prime\prime}$ indicates an interpenetrated or catenated structure). SIFSIX-3-Ni and SIFSIX-3-Zn, both synthesized from pyrazine-based linkers, have shown remarkable performance toward trace $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, suggesting similar performance may be observed with comparable small molecule guest gases such as $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ .102,174 As shown in the figure, single gas $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms reveal that SIFSIX-1-Cu exhibits a very high $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $11{\mathrm{~mmol~g}}^{-1}$ at 1 bar and $298\ \mathrm{K},$ which is a higher capacity than that of either SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, SIFSIX-3-Ni, or SIFSIX- $\cdot3-\cdot\angle\mathrm{n}$ . Furthermore, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i and SIFSIX-1-Cu show remarkable $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ uptake of 2.3 and $1.8~\mathrm{mmol}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1},}\end{array}$ , respectively, at an extremely low $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ partial pressure of 2 mbar at 298 K (Figure 24). DFT calculations reveal the preferential adsorption sites, and mixture gas breakthrough studies demonstrate the potential applications of these MOFs toward the separation of $\mathrm{SO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ . Though stability of these MOFs in the presence of water vapor remains to be evaluated, these materials are promising candidates for the flue gas desulfurization process.
Finally, Eddaoudi and co-workers recently reported the use of fluorinated MOFs, NbOFFIVE-1-Ni (KAUST-7) and AlFFIVE1-Ni (KAUST-8), for the selective removal of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from flue gas and the sensing of trace amounts of $S O_{2}$ .177 Column breakthrough studies of KAUST-7 and KAUST-8 with a gas mixture $\left(\mathrm{S}\bar{\mathrm{O}}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}=7/93\right)$ revealed a good $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ uptake capacity of ${\sim}2.2\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in both cases. Moreover, $S O_{2}$ uptake by these MOFs is maintained when the MOFs are exposed to a gas mixture containing only $500\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2},$ both exhibiting $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ uptake values of $1.4\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under these conditions. Importantly, this MOF platform is stable toward humid $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ conditions. A combination of DFT calculations and single crystal X-ray diffraction studies on $S O_{2}$ -loaded MOF crystals elucidated the interaction of $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules with electronegative fluorine atoms of the adjacent anionic pillars and hydrogens of pyrazines (Figure 25). Additional mixed gas breakthrough experiments illustrate a significant enhancement in the $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ /host interaction energy for KAUST-8 compared to KAUST-7 $\left\langle{S\mathrm{O}_{2}}\right\rangle$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ values of ${\sim}66$ and ${\sim}1$ , respectively). Lastly, quartz crystal microbalance (QCM)-based sensors with fluorinated KAUST-7 and KAUST-8 are capable of sensing $S\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from air.
## 3.4. Carbon Monoxide (CO)
Also known as the silent killer, carbon monoxide is a colorless, odorless gas, and inadvertent exposure to CO can be potentially fatal due to CO’s nearly 200-times stronger coordination than $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to hemoglobin, which essentially deprives cells from oxygen.186 Exposures to ${>}70~\mathrm{ppm}$ of CO result in observable symptoms, and inhaling levels of $150{-}200~\mathrm{ppm}$ often results in disorientation or unconsciousness and can sometimes lead to death.187 More than 400 people in the U.S. die every year from carbon monoxide poisoning according to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. On the other hand, carbon monoxide is an important chemical feedstock for the synthesis of a variety of chemical commodities such as oxo-alcohols, aldehydes, esters, and organic acids.105,188 Moreover, high purity CO (i.e., $>99.99\%$ ) serves as a reference material for calibration service systems, electronics, and semiconductor devices.189 Therefore, the safe detection and storage of CO is of vital importance to scientific community. A majority of efforts toward catalytic oxidation or separation of CO use conventional porous materials such as activated carbon and zeolites; however, this review will focus on MOF-based solutions for CO capture and purification applications.190,191
HKUST-1, $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{b}\mathfrak{t}\mathfrak{c})_{2}$ ( $\mathtt{b t c}=1,3,5$ benzene, tricarboxylate), is a well-studied MOF for small gas separation applications largely due to an abundance of open metal sites in the framework.46,194 However, $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ open metal sites in HKUST-1 showed much weaker interactions with CO compared to MOF-74 analogues since HKUST-1 has smaller contribution of $\pi$ -backbonding than MOF-74.179 To increase the affinity of $\mathtt{C u}$ open metal sites with CO, Kitagawa designed a flexible MOF, $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\mathrm{aip})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , composed of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -paddlewheel sites and 5-azidoisophthalate (aip), where removal of coordinated water molecules resulted in paddle-wheel chains.182 In the resulting structure, coordination of CO introduced a reversible structural change, which resulted in high CO uptake. In the same system, $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ molecules did not induce a structural change due to nitrogen’s weak interaction with open metal sites. As a result, $85\%$ and $94\%\mathrm{N}_{2}$ enrichments were achieved after the first and second cycles, respectively, for an equimolar $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixture.
In addition to utilizing the open metal sites present on the nodes of a MOF, adsorption sites can be introduced through postmetalation of the framework for selective adsorption of CO molecules. For instance, MIL-100−Fe and MIL-101−Cr have been modified via postmetalation to introduce Cu1+ sites.181,195
The uptake of CO in the MOF was significantly improved after metalation owing to $\pi$ -complexation between CO and $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ . The Cu-impregnated MIL-101−Cr was regenerated at room temperature under vacuum condition, and $90\%$ of its initial CO uptake was maintained even after exposure to air for a month.180
Trapping gases inside the pores of MOFs (sieving) is another route to increasing the overall capacity of CO in MOFs. However, weak intermolecular interactions, in general, result in rapid desorption of these gases upon pressure drop. A recent study on MOF-74 analogues showed that introducing ethylenediamine (EDA) after adsorption can retain a variety of weakly adsorbing molecules, including CO, inside the MOF by forming a capping layer at the external surface of microcrystals. While one end of EDA coordinates to the metal, the other end of six EDA molecules blocks the pore entrance by forming a hydrogenbonded network. Time-resolved infrared spectra showed that CO molecules inside the pores can be evacuated in parent NiMOF-74 as early as $30~\mathrm{min}$ , while EDA-modified Ni-MOF-74 maintained more than $97\%$ of the CO from the pores upon evacuation for $2\mathrm{h}$ .196 Notably, EDA concentration and exposure time require careful experimental design to limit the EDA layer to the surface of the MOF. Otherwise, EDA can occupy the open metal sites in MOF since amines can bind to the metal sites much stronger than CO, which would lower the CO uptake especially at lower pressures. Combined with ab initio calculations, this spectroscopic study showed that water can be introduced to release the trapped gas molecules, providing a route to access new reversible CO storage materials.
Although open metal sites were shown to be promising design factors for materials with efficient $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{\bar{C}O}/\mathrm{\bar{H}}_{2}$ separations, $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation is relatively challenging. Rather than relying on Lewis acidic sites to separate $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ differences in kinetic diameter and polarizability can be exploited to design materials with selective uptake for one gas over the other. Chen et al. utilized hybrid ultramicroporous materials (HUMs) namely SIFSIX-3-Ni and TIFSIX-2-Cu-i, which were constructed from $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ or $\mathrm{TiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ , dipyridyl organic linkers, and divalent transition metals resulting in pcu network topology with narrow micropores.197 Ultramicropores and strong electrostatics within the pores resulted in very high selectivity (reaching to 4000) toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over CO due to smaller kinetic diameter and larger polarizability of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ compared to CO. Dynamic separation experiments showed successful removal of both trace $(1\%)$ and bulk $(50\%)$ 1 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from CO resulting in ultrahigh purity $\left(>99.99\%\right)$ CO gas (Figure 27).
Because of the higher polarizability of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ than CO, functionalization of the linker with strongly polarizing groups can be used to favor $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake and generate materials with higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}$ selectivities. Yaghi and co-workers have reported multivariate (MTV) MOF-5 analogues with substituted 1,4-benzodicarboylate linkers containing functional groups such as ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2},{\mathrm{NO}}_{2},$ Br, or Cl.198 As expected, the uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ increased while the CO uptake decreased due to lower surface area of the functionalized materials. Surprisingly, they reported that the multivariate MOFs led to properties that are not simply linear sums of those of the pure component MOFs; reaching up to $400\%$ better selectivity compared to MOFs built from individual linkers, highlighting the importance of synergetic effects in gas separation systems. In a similar manner, amine-functionalized titanium MIL-125 $(\mathrm{MIL}.125–\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ $\left(\mathrm{Ti}_{8}{\mathrm O}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{bdc})_{6}\right)$ has also been evaluated for CO purification by using a combination of single gas isotherms, column breakthrough, and pressure-swing adsorption (PSA) experiments, and it showed uptake 0.22 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ CO at $303\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar.185 However, this study did not report CO uptake of parent MIL-125 for comparison.
## 3.5. Nitrogen Oxides $(\mathsf{N O}_{x})$
Highly toxic nitrogen oxides $(\mathrm{NO}_{x})$ are generated from major anthropogenic sources such as coal, oil, and natural gas burning, automobile exhausts, and agricultural activities.199,200 The most prevalent forms of $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ in the atmosphere are nitric oxide (NO) and nitrogen dioxide $\left(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)$ . Exposure to high levels of $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ causes both eye and skin irritation as well as respiratory damage. Beyond the species’ toxicities, $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ in the presence of sunlight generates acid rain, a significant environmental concern.201 Additionally, $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ species contribute to the generation of photochemical smog and ozone at ground level. According to a U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) report, around 1 million tons of $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ in one year was produced from coal fired plants despite the presence of NOx-removal technologies.202 Thus, renewed efforts have been focused on $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ removal and catalytic conversion technologies. While in large amounts it is hazardous to humans, NO plays a crucial role in various cardiovascular, nervous, and immunological systems.203 Therefore, the design of the materials for NO uptake is necessary under specific biological conditions. To date, activated carbon, graphite oxides, iron composites, zeolites, and titania have been used as $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ adsorbents. However, these materials face limitations such as low adsorption capacities and irreversible $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ uptake via disproportionation to $\mathrm{NO^{+}}$ and ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ . Another strategy to remove $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ is the selective catalytic reduction (SCR) of $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ to $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ in the presence of ammonia, urea, and hydrocarbons from stationary sources.204 However, the practical implementation of this process is limited by the deactivation of basic metal oxide $\left(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}{-}\mathrm{WO}_{3}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ catalysts in the presence of water and the use of highly corrosive $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ as a reducing agent.
A growing interest in the development of new adsorbent materials that remove $\mathrm{NO}_{x},$ rather than convert the species, is due to the utility of $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ in certain industries. $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ is widely used in chemical industries as an inhibitor in acrylate polymerization211 as well as a nitrating or bleaching agent.212 Moreover, NO has been implemented as a therapeutic.213 Thus, the potential applications of $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ motivated the design materials that both adsorb $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ and deliver $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ under specific conditions. Over the past two decades, MOFs have emerged as a revolutionary functional material based on host−guest interactions and have been subsequently implemented for $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ capture and release.
Seminal work has showed the efficacy of several MOFs for $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ uptake. Cu-based framework HKUST-1 reported an uptake capacity of 2.30 and 1.17 mmol $\mathrm{NO}_{2}/\mathrm{g}$ MOF under dry and $70\%$ humidity conditions, respectively, at room temperature. A graphite oxide composite of HKUST-1, $\mathrm{GO-}$ HKUST-1, displayed a capacity of $2.43{-}2.91\ \mathrm{mmol}$ $\mathrm{NO}_{2}/\mathrm{g}$ MOF under dry condition and $0.83{-}1.28~\mathrm{mmol/g}$ under $70\%$ humidity (Table 7).205 PXRD indicated the likely decomposition of these materials after contact with $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ while a $90\%$ and $80\%$ reduction in BET surface area was observed by HKUST-1 and GO-HKUST-1, respectively. MOF degradation was attributed to a reaction between the Cu node and ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2},$ forming a bidentate nitrate observed by differential thermogravimetric analysis and infrared spectroscopy. Another MOF, $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - based framework UiO-66 and UiO-67, exhibited respective uptakes of 1.59 and $1.72~\mathrm{\mmol}$ $\mathrm{NO}_{2}/\mathrm{g}$ MOF under dry condition.206 Under $71\%$ humidity, $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ uptake was enhanced to $2.56\mathrm{mmol/g}$ due to $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ dissolution in water. However, both MOFs collapsed after exposure to $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ as suggested by a decreased in BET surface area and a loss in crystallinity. Additional work probed linker modification in UiO-66 by investigating $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ which captured $20.3\ \mathrm{mmol/g\NO_{2}}$ under dry conditions, while $31.2\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ was adsorbed under $80\%$ humidity.208 This impressive uptake was significantly higher than the theoretical value predicted by its crystal structure and conventional adsorption theory. The mechanism of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ uptake by $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was probed through characterization techniques including infrared, NMR, and XPS spectroscopy; the studies suggested a pathway involving the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group to produce diazonium ions. McGrath et al. observed that $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ removal occurred through the release of $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ gas concurrent with the formation of phenol from aryl diazonium salt.214 Framework integrity and crystallinity were maintained during bond cleavage in spite of chemical reactions occurring within the framework. Further linker modifications of UiO-66 incorporated at free carboxylic acid to form UiO-66-ox. Microbreakthrough experiments indicated that UiO-66-ox captured $8.4\mathrm{\mmol/g\NO}_{2},$ a value significantly greater than UiO-66 $\underline{{3.8}}\mathrm{mmol/g}$ measured by breakthrough experiments as well).207 The vast increase in gas uptake was attributed to an interaction between the free COOH group and $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ . However, the structural decomposition was observed after exposure to $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ confirmed by PXRD. Beyond functionalizing the linker, the functionalization of the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ UiO node was explored through metal dopants. Ce(III) was postsynthetically incorporated onto UiO-66 and UiO-67, which captured $2.1\mathrm{mmol/g}$ and $1.87\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ respectively, under dry conditions while $1.15\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ and $1.85\mathrm{\mmol/g\NC}$ $\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ respectively, under $71\%$ humidity conditions.210 The adsorption of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ by ${\mathrm{Ce}}({\mathrm{III}})$ -doped UiO-66 and UiO-67 was higher than that of the respective parents due to the presence of additional adsorption sites. However, PXRD showed poor structural stability upon exposure of the ${\mathrm{Ce}}({\mathrm{III}})$ -doped frameworks to $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ . Despite their acid and moisture stability, UiO-66 frameworks exhibit partial deterioration due to the reaction of NO2 at the Zr−O sites.206
Therefore, strategies to improve MOF structural stability and reusability for $\Nu{\cal O}_{2}$ adsorption have been developed. A $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF $[\mathrm{Z\dot{r}_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(F A)\dot{_{6}}}]_{2}(\mathrm{cal)_{3}}]$ ( $\mathrm{FA}=$ formate, $_\mathrm{cal}=1,3$ alte, Calin linketetrakis[(carboxy)methoxy]calixarene) incorporated calixarenes as linkers due to their unique charge transfer reaction with $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ at room temperature, creating reversible colorimetric $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ sensors215 (Figure 28). This charge transfer occurred through the partial ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ formation and its disproportionation to $\mathrm{NO^{+}}$ and ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ . The purple color charge transfer complex formed due to the encapsulation of $\mathrm{NO^{+}}$ molecule as well as the coordination between ${\mathrm{{\bar{N}O_{3}}}}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ sites as confirmed by UV−vis and IR spectra. Most notably, the MOF crystallinity was retained during repeated $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ exposure, yet quantification of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ adsorption was not mentioned. Another example of reversible, high $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ capture was observed in the highly robust MFM-300(Al) framework.209 Beyond an uptake capacity of 14.1 $\mathrm{mmol/g},$ an unprecedented selective removal of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ in lowconcentrations $\left(<1\mathrm{ppm}\right)$ was demonstrated as shown in Figure 29. Furthermore, MFM-300(Al) selectively adsorbed $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ with 18.1 over ${\mathrm{~\thinspace~}}S{\mathrm{O}}_{2},$ 248 over $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $>1000$ over $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at room temperature and 1.0 bar. Synchrotron PXRD, infrared spectroscopy, and EPR spectroscopy as well as density function theory and molecular dynamics calculations supported reversible $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ adsorption in the robust framework. A strong interaction between porous hosts and guest molecules was observed in MFM- 300(Al) via the formation of a $\left(\mathrm{NO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\right)_{\infty}$ monomer−dimer 1D helical chain. Additionally, five cycles of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ sorption and desorption demonstrated the impressive structural stability of MFM-300(Al). Full reversibility in MFM300(Al) was observed post $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ adsorption, allowing the captured analyte to be used in a potential variety of applications. Recently, Nenoff reported a robust MOF with rare earth metal (RE-DOBDC) that captured $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ gases under $60\%$ relative humidity.216 Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy and thermogravimetric−mass spectrometry analyses in tandem with computational modeling supported $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ binding with the metal in the framework. Moreover, the structure was fully retained after $24\mathrm{h}$ exposure of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ as observed by PXRD. The guest−framework interactions were probed by investigating photoluminescence properties under visible light, which induced a distinct color change.
NO storage and selective release from the MOFs have been studied for biological applications, and open metal sites (OMS)
within MOFs are considered to be prerequisites for NO adsorption.217,218 For example, the adsorption of the pharmacologically active NO was investigated over a series of highly pliable MIL-88 type iron(III) dicarboxylate MOFs and achieved around $1{-}2.5~\mathrm{mmol~g^{-1}}$ in uptake.217 As iron is less toxic and more environmentally friendly, MIL-88 type MOFs could be appropriate for use in NO treatment. According to IR spectroscopy, NO was adsorbed on either accessible $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ or $\hat{\mathrm{Fe}}^{2+}$ sites via a chemical reaction, or physisorbs within the accessible pore of MOFs. Containing both Lewis and Bronsted acidic sites, another iron-based framework MIL-100-Fe exhibited excellent activity for the selective catalytic reduction of NO.219 Alternatively, copper containing HKUST-1 captured ${\sim}9$ mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ NO at 296 K (1.0 bar), among the highest adsorption capacities of porous materials.220 Such performance was attributed to a strong interaction between NO and $\mathtt{C u}$ ions in the framework, as suggested by the IR spectroscopy. While only $2\ \mu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of NO was released, a loss of material crystallinity was observed. Through the incorporation of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ into HKUST-1, NO release increased to $65\mu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . However, minimal improvement in the framework stability was observed. The high density of OMS within the MOF-74 series has attracted great attention from researchers. Ni-MOF-74 and CuMOF-74 adsorbed 7.0 mmol $\mathrm{{NO}/g}$ of activated material at room temperature. These materials with NO bound can be stored for several months, which is desirable for medical applications. Adsorption and powder X-ray diffraction patterns supported direct coordination of the NO molecule to the metal atom, forming a 1:1 adduct.220,221 Moreover, the MOFs were fully recovered without any structural changes after the release of NO initiated by flowing humid air. However, $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ are not biocompatible, limiting their implementations for medical application. Although biocompatible materials can be accessed through transmetalation of MOF-74 with $\mathrm{{Mg}(I I)}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , NO bonded too strongly with $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ and too weakly with Zn ions. Thus, the rate of NO delivery from Mg-MOF-74 was too slow, whereas Zn-MOF-74 released NO too quickly. However, select doping of Ni into the biocompatible MgMOF-74 framework was explored to increase uptake. The $40\%$ $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ doped Mg-MOF-74 achieved the desired amount of adsorption and delivery of NO.222 Efforts returned to higher performing iron MOFs through the synthesis of Fe-MOF-74, which captured $6.21\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}\ N}$ O at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ (7 mbar). Such uptake suggested that ${\sim}95\%$ of Fe(II) OMS were occupied by NO.223 Neutron diffraction studies observed a strong binding of NO to unsaturated, redox-active ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{II})$ within Fe-MOF-74, forming $\mathrm{Fe(III){-}N O}$ adducts. Moreover, the oxidation of the ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{II})$ sites corresponded with an observed distinct color change from a bright green MOF to dark brown upon exposure to NO. Under a flow of $11\%$ humidity in ${\bf N}_{2},$ the bound NO is continuously released over a 10 day period.223 Extending NO uptake to another framework with iron OMS, ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{II})$ exchanged MOF-5, disproportionated NO to generate $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and a ferric nitrito complex (Figure 30).224 In situ diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) and diffuse reflectance UV−vis/NIR spectroscopy revealed a direct NO interaction with Fe ions via a Fe−N bond. This study reported that the single site of a Fe node in Fe-MOF-5 offers a platform for investigating N−N bond forming transformations.
## 4. CONCLUSIONS AND OUTLOOK
Adsorptive removal of toxic chemicals for personal protection and air purification has been of specific interest to the scientific community for many years. With the discovery of MOFs, there is an ever-increasing body of work for designing materials selectively toward targeted gases due to the unmatched tunability offered by MOFs relative to that of other traditional porous materials. The development of chemically stable MOFs that can maintain the framework integrity upon exposure to moisture and corrosive gases has further motivated researchers to design MOFs employed for the capture of CWAs and TICs.
While tuning the pore sizes and shapes in MOFs can be an efficient strategy to enable the adsorption of some gases, this approach cannot be generalized to adsorb toxic chemicals, and as a result, decorating the MOF pores with strong adsorption sites is needed. Introducing open-metal sites for enhanced interactions with targeted Lewis basic gases has been shown to be a promising strategy to capture gases, especially when these gases are present at low concentrations. Open-metal sites can be introduced via the native form of the MOF node, by tuning the defects in the structure, or postmetalation strategies. Additionally, decorating the pore walls of MOFs with polar groups has been shown to increase the uptake of polar gases, like ammonia, via hydrogen bonding or ionic interactions, while amine functionalities have been shown to react selectively with $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ gases.
Molecular simulation has proved to be instrumental in the identification of promising MOFs for gas capture and storage applications including toxic gases. Improvement in the accuracy of models for predicting the competition between water and toxic gas molecules, while reducing the computing time, would significantly reduce experimental effort needed and therefore potentially accelerate the materials discovery of MOFs used for toxic gas capture.
Although single component gas uptake studies under dynamic settings are sufficient to demonstrate the affinity and uptake capacity of the sorbets, multicomponent studies using breakthrough apparatuses are vital to demonstrate selectivity under relevant conditions. Importantly, testing the performance of the MOFs under moist conditions is crucial because a majority of the proposed applications will be employed under atmospheric conditions with humidity present. Therefore, understanding the competition between water and the gas of interest is crucial. In turn, materials that exhibit selectivity for analyte adsorption onto open-metal sites or polar functional groups in MOFs over water are especially important. On the other hand, the high solubility of ammonia and $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ in water has been shown to be advantageous, resulting in MOFs that exhibit higher uptake capacities in the presence of moisture compared to dry conditions.
Saving a human life with a MOF-based personal mask or filter can be considered a success, and the regeneration of the exposed mask may not be necessary. However, chemical stability and regenerability of MOFs under demanding and relevant conditions are essential for economically viable industrial filters for exhausts. Although PXRD analysis of MOFs after sorption has been utilized as a diagnostic method of demonstrating chemical stability, PXRD patterns alone cannot be taken as a proof of stability. Unless there is a phase change to another form of a crystalline framework upon exposure to toxic gases, there would not be a significant difference in PXRD pattern (i.e., no change in the PXRD pattern would be observed even if roughly $80\%$ of the MOF was transformed to an amorphous coordination polymer). Therefore, quantitative techniques that are sensitive to the porosities, such as ${\bf N}_{2}$ isotherms and adsorption studies, should be also performed to establish structural integrity upon exposure to the toxic gases.225
While the vast majority of the work on MOFs has been performed using microcrystalline powders as a proof-of-concept, actual protective equipment would require shaped particles that maintain the intrinsic properties of the powders.226,227 To this end, the pelletization, or extrusion, of MOFs has emerged as an instrumental technique.228 Even so, the mechanical stability of MOFs under relevant conditions should be evaluated to establish design rules that encompass the balance of adsorbate uptake and stability, ultimately helping the community understand the trade-offs involved in selecting the optimal materials for toxic gas capture.229,230 Theoretical and experimental works on the mechanical stability of MOFs are rare relative to other studies on MOFs; however, recent promising studies have demonstrated conclusive trends that can be observed in MOF families, which will potentially support the further development of the field.231 The integration of MOFs onto fiber textiles is another approach in which MOFs can be used as protective layers in personal protective equipment. In these systems, fibers can remove aerosols, while the MOFs can act as a reactive and adsorptive layer for toxic chemicals. We and others have reported successful examples of MOF@fiber composite materials that effectively captured and destroyed toxic chemicals or their simulants.44,45,85,232 Further development in this area will allow for the faster transformation of MOFs from the lab bench to an industrially relevant product.
