## Biographies
Timur Islamoglu is a research associate in the Farha group at Northwestern University where he was a postdoctoral researcher previously (2016−2017). His main research interest is the design and synthesis of MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts. He also puts efforts toward understanding the underlying MOF growth process. He received his M.Sc. (2013) and Ph.D. (2016) in Chemistry from Virginia Commonwealth University.
Zhijie Chen was born in Fuzhou, Fujian, China. He received B.S. (2012) degree from Shanghai Jiao Tong University (SJTU) in China under the supervision of Prof. Yong Cui, and Ph.D. (2018) from King Abdullah University of Science and Technology (KAUST) under the supervision of Prof. Mohamed Eddaoudi. He joined Prof. Omar Farha’s group at Northwestern University as a postdoctoral researcher in 2018. His research interest is related to the reticular synthesis of functional porous materials as well as their structure−property relationships.
Megan C. Wasson was born and raised in Pittsburgh, PA. She received her B.S. in Chemistry in 2017 from Duquesne University, where she worked on copper-catalyzed atom transfer radical addition under Prof. Tomislav Pintauer. She is currently a Ph.D. Candidate and an NSF graduate research fellow in Prof. Omar K. Farha’s research group at Northwestern University. Her research interests are related to the synthesis and design of metal−organic frameworks for heterogeneous catalysis.
Cassandra T. Buru earned her Ph.D. in inorganic chemistry from Northwestern University in 2019 under the supervision of professors Omar K. Farha and Joseph T. Hupp. She previously earned her B.S./ M.S. from Emory University in 2015. Her doctoral thesis focused on using metal−organic frameworks and their polyoxometalate composites as catalysts for chemical warfare agent degradation.
Kent O. Kirlikovali was born and raised in Southern California. He received a B.S. in Chemistry from USC in 2014, where he studied luminescent zinc dipyrrin complexes in the laboratory of Prof. Mark Thompson. Kent obtained his Ph.D. from the University of California, Los Angeles in 2019 under the supervision of Prof. Alex Spokoyny where he developed phosphorescent $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\operatorname{Ir}(\operatorname{III})$ complexes with carborane-based ligands. He joined Prof. Omar Farha’s research group in 2019 as an International Institute of Nanotechnology (IIN) Postdoctoral Fellow, and his research is focused on creating MOFbased luminescent sensors used for the detection of chemical warfare agents.
Mohammad Rasel Mian is currently a postdoctoral researcher in the Department of Chemistry at Northwestern University in Prof. Omar K. Farha’s group. He received his B.S. degree from University of Dhaka.
He earned his M.S. (2015) and Ph.D. (2018), via a MEXT and JSPS, in Chemistry from Tohoku University under the supervision of Prof. M. Yamashita and Prof. S. Takaishi. His current research is focusing on metal−organic frameworks and their applications in catalysis.
Unjila Afrin was born in Dhaka, Bangladesh. She received her B.S. and M.S. in Chemistry from University of Dhaka. She earned her second M.S. and Ph.D. in Chemistry from Tohoku University, Japan under the supervision of Prof. Masahiro Yamashita where she held a Japanese Government (MEXT) Fellowship. Currently she is a visiting scholar at the Department of Chemistry, Northwestern University with Omar K. Farha. Her research interest is on the design and synthesis of MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts.
Omar K. Farha is a professor of Chemistry at Northwestern University, president of NuMat Technologies, and Associate Editor for ACS Applied Materials & Interfaces. His current research spans diverse areas of chemistry and materials science ranging from energy to defense related challenges. His research accomplishments have been recognized by several awards and honors including the Japanese Society of Coordination Chemistry “International award for creative work”; the Royal Society of Chemistry “Environment, Sustainability and Energy Division Early Career” Award; and the American Chemical society “The Satinder Ahuja Award for Young Investigators in Separation Science”.
